 State GUI props;master;canvas;mask;icon;points;size;origin;t;new
⍝ Create a GUI drawing object on the State.target bitmap
⍝ Do this the hard way if Windowing or Clipping is in effect, otherwise just draw it directly
⍝ Special Icon "object" property syntax: 'Icon' ('Icon' objname) ('Points' rc)
⍝ Special Text object property syntax when clipped: 'Text' ('Text' ...)('Points' rc) ... ('Size' hw)

 :If (0=≢State.Window)∧0=≢State.Clip
 :AndIf 'Icon'≢⊃props                                       ⍝ But we have to use the special clipping code to do Icons
 :AndIf ~('Text'≡⊃props)∧'Size'⊂⍛∊⊃¨props                   ⍝ And clipped Text also needs to be done with the special code
     {}(State NewName State.target)##.∆WC props             ⍝ Just do it the easy way most of the time
     State._dirty←1
     :Return
 :EndIf

 ⍝ Now for the much more complicated (and slower) method...
 'This procedure only works on Bitmap objects!'##.Halt'Bitmap'≢⊃State.target ##.∆WGet⊂'Type' ''
 origin←0 0                                                 ⍝ Start out by assuming it's measuring from (0,0)
 master←State.target ##.∆WG'CBits'                          ⍝ Get the pixels from the master Bitmap for us to end up changing
 'Shouldn''t be using floating-point storage'##.Halt~##.IsInt master
 canvas←State._Owner⍕⍛,'.',##.Next State._Owner             ⍝ Where we can create temporary Bitmaps & Icons
 canvas←⎕VGET canvas ##.∆WC'Bitmap'('CBits'master)          ⍝ Create a temporary copy of the master pixels for direct modification
 mask←master⍴⍛⍴1                                            ⍝ Start building us a mask to represent the area we're allowed to actually change
 :If ×≢State.Window
     origin←2↑State.Window ⋄ mask∧←State.Window_Mask        ⍝ Limit area of concern and starting point based on current Window setting (if any)
 :EndIf
 :If ×≢State.Clip ⋄ mask∧←State.Clip_Mask ⋄ :EndIf          ⍝ Further limit area based on current Clip setting (if any)

 :Section                                                   ⍝ Draw graphical objects differently for certain Types
     :Select ⊃props                                         ⍝ It's time to create the drawing object on the working canvas
     :Case 'Icon'
         ⍝ Let's pick up the only properties we care about, and we'll use a pre-defined Icon object name instead of a File name
         (icon points)←props ##.Classes._supp.GetInitialProps'Icon' 'Points'
         points←##.Single points                            ⍝ It will usually be nested
         props←StartObj'Image'                              ⍝ We'll create an unnamed Image object to output the Icon for us
         props,←⊂'Points'⊂⍛,points+origin                   ⍝ The size will always be 32×32 for APL+Win
         props,←⊂'Picture'icon
         {}(State NewName canvas)##.∆WC props               ⍝ Now write the Icon onto the working canvas
         mask∧←mask⍴⍛↑(-32+points+origin)↑32 32⍴1           ⍝ Modify the mask array to show where the Icon was drawn
         ##.∆EX icon                                        ⍝ Get rid of our temporary Icon
     :Case 'Text'
         ⍝ Extract the [pseudo-]properties we're going use to mask off the maximum size
         (points size)←props ##.Classes._supp.GetInitialProps'Points' 'Size'
         points←##.Single points                            ⍝ It will usually be nested
         :If 2=≢size
             mask∧←mask⍴⍛↑(-points+origin)↑size⍴1           ⍝ Modify the mask array to show where we're limiting the Text output
         :EndIf
         t←(⊃¨props)⍳⍥⎕C⊂'Points'
         (t⊃props)←'Points'⊂⍛,origin+points                 ⍝ Adjust Points for a relocated Window origin
         props/⍨←~(⊃¨props)∊⊂'Size'                         ⍝ Exclude the pseudo-property from what we give ∆WC
         {}(State NewName canvas)##.∆WC props               ⍝ Now we can write the Text object directly onto the working canvas normally
     :Else                                                  ⍝ Most drawing objects are handled here and just respect Window & Clip settings
         'Don''t all GUI objects use Points?'##.Halt props≢⍛<t←(⊃¨props)⍳⍥⎕C⊂'Points'
         (t⊃props)←'Points'⊂⍛,origin+##.Single 1↓t⊃props    ⍝ Adjust Points (usually nested) for a relocated Window origin
         {}(State NewName canvas)##.∆WC props               ⍝ Just write the drawing object directly onto the working canvas normally
     :EndSelect
 :EndSection

 new←canvas ##.∆WG'CBits'                                   ⍝ Capture the full-size working image; use separate line to minimize ⎕WA usage
 'Shouldn''t be using floating-point storage'##.Halt~##.IsInt new
 ##.∆EX⍕canvas                                              ⍝ Clean up our working area as early as possible
 mask←,mask ⋄ new←,new                                      ⍝ Try to conserve ⎕WA during single-line computations
 new←mask/new                                               ⍝ Select only the masked bits from the working image, with minimal ⎕WA usage
 size←⍴master ⋄ master←,master                              ⍝ Continue trying to conserve ⎕WA
⍝? How can we selectively assign the new bits into the master with minimal ⎕WA? I don't even want to try looping (way too slow)
 mask←⍸mask                                                 ⍝ (This way doesn't seem to save any more space than: (mask/master)←new)
 master←new@mask⊢master                                     ⍝ Store the new bits directly into the masked area of the final image
 new←mask←0                                                 ⍝ Reclaim no-longer-needed ⎕WA to keep overhead as low as possible
 master←size⍴master                                         ⍝ Restore our natural shape
 State.target ##.∆WS'CBits'master                           ⍝ Replace our target Bitmap with the new data
 State._dirty←1
